VERDICT: UPHOLD
CHECKER: judge-standards
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: R5
ATTEMPT: 2
---
Independently reproduced the agreed fact before ruling: on a scratch copy,
swapping accounts.go:307's `asOf.Location()` for `time.Now().Location()`
leaves `go test ./internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger/...` green under both
TZ=America/New_York and TZ=Asia/Tokyo. Confirmed.

The brief's "Attempt 2" section has two separate evidence obligations, and
they must be read separately:

1. "A test pinning the today/tomorrow edge that FAILS under a non-UTC TZ if
   the boundary is wrong. Run it under at least two TZ values."
2. "Mutation check per fix, on a scratch copy outside the repo."

On (1): the text is genuinely ambiguous as to whether it covers the
explicit-as_of path. Evidence-bullet 1 explicitly says "A default-path
test"; bullet 2 drops that qualifier. Read against the surrounding
narrative -- the numbered defect list states "Explicit as_of is unaffected
[by the off-by-one bug]... which is exactly why the committed tests miss
it" -- the more natural referent for bullet 2 is bug 3, which is
default-path-only, and the delivered
TestGetBalanceProjectionDefaultAsOfReferenceAmountStableAcrossTimezones
satisfies that reading. I do not resolve this ambiguity against the worker:
where the lead's text underspecifies which path a requirement binds, the
run bears that cost, not the worker who satisfied a defensible reading.

On (2), however, the text is NOT ambiguous. "Required fix" enumerates three
things: the as_of-absent branch, the as_of-present branch, and,
"Additionally, normalise as_of to a day boundary ... so the truncation
boundary and BalanceAt's boundary agree." That normalisation is squarely
one of the enumerated fixes, and it lives entirely on the explicit-as_of
path (accounts.go:300-309) -- it is the one piece of Attempt-2-required
code the "default path" reading of bullet 1 cannot cover. "Mutation check
per fix" carries no path qualifier and no exception; on its plain text it
binds this fix too.

The worker's own code comment at accounts.go:303-306 states the
normalisation "is a no-op in practice ... here so the boundary is correct
by construction rather than by the parse format's coincidence" -- i.e., the
worker knew the guarantee is currently unreachable-input-only and wrote the
line specifically to be correct independent of that coincidence. A mutation
check "per fix" for a line whose entire stated purpose is defense against a
Location-source mistake has to test a Location-source mistake. checker-tests'
own Criterion 4 mutation (removing the normalisation line entirely) only
proves the fix is currently a no-op given reachable inputs -- it does not
exercise the failure mode the fix exists to prevent, and is not equivalent
to "mutation check per fix" in the sense the brief's other mutation
checks use the phrase (Criterion 6's mutations each flip a real branch and
show a committed test catches the resulting wrong output). checker-second's
mutation is the direct analogue for this fix: it flips the Location source,
which is exactly the bug pattern (a boundary built in the wrong Location,
disagreeing with BalanceAt's boundary in a non-UTC TZ) that this entire
attempt exists to close -- one call frame away from where attempt 1 was
faulted for it. No committed test catches it, confirmed above.

Because "mutation check per fix" is unambiguous text and is not satisfied
for the normalisation fix regardless of how bullet 1's scope is read, the
Evidence-required list as written is not fully met. checker-second's FAIL
is correct on the standard as written; the ambiguity in bullet 1 does not
rescue the shortfall in bullet 2, which has no comparable ambiguity.

Ruling: UPHOLD. FAIL stands; the task returns to the worker. The
outstanding gap is narrow and should be stated precisely in the next
dispatch: add a committed test (any TZ-sensitive fixture where an explicit
as_of's day boundary, if built in a non-UTC-derived Location, would
include/exclude a different set of rows than BalanceAt's own boundary) that
fails under the swapped-Location mutation reproduced here, run under at
least two TZ values, alongside the existing default-path pin.
